define r = Character("Rylie")
define e = Character("Earl")

label c2_scene:

    play music "audio/C2BGAudio.mp3" volume 0.35 loop

    scene bg FarmYard
    play sound "audio/BirdEffect.mp3" loop

    "The air outside feels cool and open after the tight warmth of the kitchen."
    
    "A thin mist hangs low over the yard, stretched between the barn and the fence line."
    
    "The farm opens up in every direction from here: chicken coop to the left, the garden plot past it, the pig pen beyond the second fence,"
    
    "and down at the far end where the land starts its slow slope toward the tree line, the horse stalls."
    
    "She's been on this land long enough that she doesn't have to think about the routine anymore."
    
    "It runs on its own in the back of her mind each morning, and her feet already know where to go."
    
    "She steps off the porch. The grass is wet against her boots."
    
    "A crow drops down onto the fence post a few feet ahead, tilts its head and looks at her with that flat, unbothered crow expression,"
    
    "then lifts off without a sound."
    
    "She watches it go over the tree line and doesn't think much of it."
    
    "Then she heads toward the chicken coop."

    scene bg ChickenBG with fade
    
    play sound "audio/ChickenEffect.mp3" loop

    "She hears it before she even opens the door… louder than usual, a tight, jumpy energy coming through the wood."
    
    "When she pulls it open, the hens are bunched against the far wall, shifting and pressing into each other,"
    
    "none of them settled in the nesting boxes the way they should be at this hour."
    
    "Rylie steps in slowly and fills the feed trough with the measured scoop she knows by heart,"
    
    "keeping her movements easy and unbothered so she doesn't add to whatever has them riled."
    
    "Gradually they drift toward the food. One, then two, then most of the group."
    
    "The routine of eating pulls them back the way it always does."
    
    "She collects the eggs, seven today and is almost to the door when she notices the one in the far corner."
    
    "Head low, feathers puffed up tight around herself. Not eating, not moving toward the others."
    
    "Just sitting there, pressed into the wall, looking like something she can't shake loose."
    
    "Rylie sets the basket down and crouches in front of her. She looks over carefully, checks the eyes, the feet, and runs a gentle hand along the feathers."
    
    "No visible injury. No swelling. Nothing she can name."
    
    stop sound
    
    "She picks the basket back up and heads out. She'll come back to check at midday. Sometimes animals just have bad nights. It happens."

    scene bg PigPen with fade
    
    play sound "audio/PigEffect.mp3" loop
    
    stop music

    "The pigs are straightforward about everything, which she appreciates."
    
    "They're hungry, they're loud about it the second they see her coming,"
    
    "and when she gets the feed over the rail they go at it without any drama."
    
    stop sound

    scene bg FarmYard with fade
    
    stop music
    
    play sound "audio/ScaryC2Audio.mp3" 

    "She leans against the fence rail while they eat and looks out over the far field."
    
    "The mist is thinning fast now, the sun is starting to find its strength,"
    
    "and the east side of the property is opening up in the light."
    
    "She can see the old split tree at the far edge…."
    
    "the one lightning hit years ago, cracking the trunk down the middle and leaving it standing like the land never bothered to finish the job."
    
    "There's something tied around the base of it."
    
    "She squints and leans forward a little, shading her eyes. Something dark, wound low around the trunk, knotted up."
    
    "Could be rope, could be some kind of cloth that blew in off the road. Hard to say from this distance."
    
    "She watches it for a moment. The pigs shuffle and push beside her. She picks up the empty bucket. She'll walk past the tree later if there's time."

    stop sound

    "The mist is gone, the sun out fully now and warm on the back of her neck as she hauls water buckets toward the garden."
    
    play music "audio/C2BGAudio.mp3" volume 0.35 loop
    
    "The farm has found its mid-morning rhythm, birds in the trees, wind working through the fence grass, the smell of the fields opening up in the heat."
    
    "She hears the truck before she sees it, tires on the gravel road, slowing, turning in through the main gate. She sets the buckets down and turns to look."
    
    "A pickup dusty, well-worked, kind of truck that's done a lot of years without complaint. The man who climbs out fits it."
    
    "Wide through the shoulders, somewhere in his fifties, sun-worn face and hands, the kind of man who's spent his whole life outside and is perfectly comfortable with that."
    
    "He takes his hat off the moment his boots hit the ground. She notices that. Some men do that and some don't."
    
    "He spots her near the fence and gives a nod."

    e "Mornin', ma'am. Hot one already, ain't it? Is Russell around? Got some business I need to go over with him."

    "His voice is polite. Careful in the way that some men get when they're trying to be respectful and aren't entirely sure of the ground they're standing on."

    r "He's inside. Back door… knock and he'll hear you."

    e "Thank you kindly. You have yourself a good mornin' now."

    "He puts his hat back on and starts toward the house. Rylie watches him for a second, then picks up her buckets."
    
    "She's seen men like him pass through several times since she came to this farm. They always come to see Russell."
    
    "They always go into the front room. And they always come back out looking sure of themselves."
    
    "Settled in some way they weren't quite settled before they went in. She's never thought much about it. Russell runs a large operation."
    
    "There are always people to talk to about it."
    
    "She carries the water to the garden and pours it slowly along the base of the plants."

    jump c3_scene